          .un_remark{:style => "border:solid #000000;background: #E6DF13;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %p
              %strong Abstract
              We discuss how the
              = succeed "-like" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
              provides a theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
              which has realizations not just in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
              but also notably in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+complex+analytic+geometry"} higher complex analytic geometry
              and moreover in “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+arithmetic+geometry"}> higher arithmetic geometry
              ” (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+arithmetic+geometry"}> E-∞ arithmetic geometry
              ) in a way that systematizes some of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogies"} analogies
              which motivate the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} geometric Langlands correspondence
          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %p
            A fruitful approach to mathematical theory is what might be called “inter-geometric”, meaning that definitions and theorems make sense and hold when interpreted in different flavors of geometry. Classical examples are the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GAGA+principle"} GAGA principle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+field+analogy"} function field analogy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} geometric
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+correspondence"} Langlands correspondence
            = succeed "-geometry" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F1"} F1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+geometry"} global analytic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogy
            between different theories of geometry has been established case-by-case, there is by and large no meta-theory which would systematically imply the analogy.
          %p
            This is of practical concern for instance in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+program"} Langlands program
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+geometry"} complex analytic geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
            = succeed "-side." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Problems+in+the+theory+of+automorphic+forms+--+45+years+later"} Langlands 14
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex-analytic+geometry"} complex-analytic geometry
            would both follow systematically.
          %p
            More generally, inter-geometric theory is relevant in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarizations"} polarizations
            correspond to descending to more rigid geometries (for introduction and review see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Schreiber14"} Schreiber 14
          %p
            A noteworthy example in this context is the construction of the refined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            in the guise of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+orientation+of+tmf"} string orientation of tmf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+geometry"} complex analytic
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"}> super
            \-)geometry is constructed by passage (via the construction of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack+of+elliptic+curves"} moduli stack of elliptic curves
            all the way down in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fracture+theorems"} fracture theorems
            by its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base changes
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+geometry"} p-adic geometry
            and to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
            (and further to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%28n%29-local+stable+homotopy+theory"} K(n)-local stable homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersingular+elliptic+curves"} supersingular elliptic curves
            which are the ones that contribute at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/height+of+a+formal+group+law"} height
            2 and hence make for the genuinely stringy (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+filtration"}> second chromatic level
            ) contribution to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+modular+forms"} refined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            exist only in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+characteristic"} positive characteristic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+string+theory"} p-adic string theory
            (closed string theory!) appearing here, which is however not of the kind that existing literature with such title would shed any light on.
          %p
            The role of more “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"}> rigid
            ”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            – closer to the bottom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borger%27s+absolute+geometry"} absolute geometry
            – in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            might be summarized in parts by the following table:
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of 3d Chern-Simons theory
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographically
              of the
              = succeed "/the" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-model"} WZW-model
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                %th
                  moduli of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
                %th
                  moduli of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarizations"} polarizations
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"}> geometric quantization
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theta+functions"} theta functions
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack+of+flat+connections"} moduli stack of flat connections
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+geometry"} complex analytic geometry
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+curve"} complex curve
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobian"}> Jacobian
                  \/moduli of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+vector+bundle"} (semi-)stable bundles
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack+of+Riemann+surfaces"} moduli stack of Riemann surfaces
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"}> modular functor
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+curve"} arithmetic curve
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+representations"} Galois representations
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphic+forms"} automorphic forms
                  (via
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+correspondence"} Langlands correspondence
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tamagawa+measures"} Tamagawa measures
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack+of+curves"} moduli stack of curves
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+elliptic+cohomology"}> equivariant elliptic cohomology
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+orientation+of+tmf"} string orientation of tmf
          %p
            The construction of the bottom right items here is a ground-breaking accomplishment in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            it maybe raises more questions than it solves: from the perspective of physics these are but the first example of a tower of higher dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            phenomena, the next instance being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            and then that of 10d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            itself (see e.g. at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 4
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (4k+3)d
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
                %th
                  form
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theta+characteristics"} theta characteristics
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 4
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (4k+2)d
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 0
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-model"}> chiral WZW-string
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"}> modular functor
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+elliptic+cohomology"} equivariant elliptic cohomology
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  self-2-2form on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 11d Chern-Simons theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
                  in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
          %p
            In all of these higher dimensional cases the inter-geometric aspect appears. Where one assigned an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology+theory"} elliptic cohomology theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheets"} worldsheets
            equipped with polarization structure, it is only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            cases of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersingular+elliptic+curves"} supersingular elliptic curves
            which contribute; similarly in the higher dimensional cases it is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin-Mazur+formal+groups"} Artin-Mazur formal groups
            in positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic"} characteristic
            which induce at the given height to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+cohomology"} Calabi-Yau cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+orientation+of+tmf"} string orientation of tmf
            for these higher dimensional cases is as desirable as it seems to be intractable without some more inter-geometric theory to guide one.
          %p Here we will not present solutions to these rather deep questions. But we do want to discuss something that looks like steps in the right direction.
          %p
            Notice that the idea of “inter-geometric theory” is ancient, it originates with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+geometry"} synthetic geometry
            of Euclid which, with the parallel axiom removed, subsumes Euclidean, elliptic and hyperbolic geometry:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+geometry"} synthetic geometry
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+geometry"} Euclidean geometry
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperbolic+geometry"} hyperbolic geometry
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+geometry"} elliptic geometry
          %p
            The idea of refining such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetic reasoning
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            is not as ancient, but far from new, this is known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differentially
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive homotopy theory
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} higher synthetic differential geometry
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+complex+analytic+geometry"} higher complex analytic geometry
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+arithmetic+geometry"} higher arithmetic geometry
          %p
            This we review and discuss
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AxiomaticDifferentialCohomology"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+field+analogy"} function field analogy
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+correspondence"} Langlands correspondence
          %h2#LanglandsAndWeil
            %strong 1)
            Langlands correspondence and Weil uniformization
          %p
            The central idea of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+correspondence"} Langlands correspondence
            (see for instance (
            %a{:href => "#Frenkel05"}> Frenkel 05
            ) for a review of the basic aspects that we refer to) is that given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+field"} global field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representations"} linear representations
            of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+group"} Galois group
            are in correspondence with certain linear representations – called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphic+representations"} automorphic representations
            – of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+linear+group"} general linear group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi GL
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL_n(\mathbb{A}_K)
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+of+adeles"} ring of adeles
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
